WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.640
How do you reduce Subset

2
00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.760
Sum to Balanced Partition? The

3
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.820
photo requires positive output integers

4
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.300
and two groups with both

5
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.520
equal sum and equal cardinality.

6
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.360
We will use a two-stage

7
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.840
construction, because ordinary Partition alone

8
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.060
does not impose the equal-number-of-entries

9
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:16.500
condition.

10
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.260
First handle easy exceptional cases

11
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.840
without solving a hard instance.

12
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.540
A zero target is a

13
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.080
yes instance using the empty

14
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.940
subset, and a target greater

15
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.580
than the total is no

16
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.840
for nonnegative inputs. Remove zero-valued

17
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.500
entries for this existence question,

18
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.480
while remembering that such removal

19
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.460
would not preserve a counting-of-solutions

20
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:36.760
question.

21
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.020
Assume the remaining target is

22
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.820
positive and at most total

23
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.940
A. Append the positive adjustment

24
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.100
absolute A minus twice t

25
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.920
when it is nonzero, producing

26
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.160
an ordinary positive Partition instance.

27
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.840
The new total is twice

28
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.160
the larger of t and

29
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.860
A minus t, so an

30
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.520
equal side must have that

31
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.220
larger sum.

32
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.760
The partition side containing the

33
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.220
adjustment has original-value sum equal

34
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.380
to the smaller of t

35
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.860
and A minus t. Such

36
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.060
a subset exists exactly when

37
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.660
a target-t subset exists, using

38
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.780
its complement when needed. If

39
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.700
the adjustment is zero, the

40
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.520
original total is twice t

41
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.020
and no extra entry is

42
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:17.560
necessary.

43
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.140
Use the authored example one,

44
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.300
two, four with target three.

45
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.440
Total seven gives adjustment one,

46
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.460
so the ordinary Partition list

47
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.680
is one, two, four, one.

48
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.540
Its equal-sum sides can be

49
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.280
the original four alone and

50
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.680
the other three entries summing

51
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:34.160
to four.

52
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.160
Now suppose that ordinary Partition

53
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.580
list has r entries. Raise

54
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.160
every b entry by one

55
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.940
and add r new entries

56
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.160
of value one, making exactly

57
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.180
two r positive entries. Require

58
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.700
the target’s two groups to

59
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.180
contain r entries each; their

60
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.860
added base-one contributions then cancel

61
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.020
when comparing sums.

62
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.760
Any balanced target solution selects

63
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.460
some raised original entries and

64
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:01.700
some dummy ones on a

65
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.020
side. Subtracting its r baseline

66
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.920
ones leaves precisely the sum

67
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.580
of the corresponding original b

68
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.440
entries, which must be half

69
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.880
the original b total. Conversely,

70
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.160
pad either side of an

71
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.940
ordinary partition with enough dummy

72
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.300
ones to reach r entries

73
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:16.620
each.

74
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.560
For our example, the output

75
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.200
is two, three, five, two,

76
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.200
then four dummy ones. One

77
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.820
balanced side is five plus

78
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.640
three dummy ones; the other

79
00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.000
is two plus three plus

80
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.420
two plus one dummy one.

81
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.460
Each side contains four entries

82
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.380
and sums to eight, so

83
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.260
both target conditions are visible.

84
00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.780
The transformation performs additions, an

85
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.980
absolute difference and linear padding.

86
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.120
Binary sums and the adjustment

87
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.900
have polynomial bit length in

88
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.820
the original encoding, so writing

89
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.680
the output is polynomial work.

90
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.300
Values that happen to be

91
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.360
equal are still different indexed

92
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.820
entries, and positivity is guaranteed

93
00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.980
by the plus-one stage.

94
00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.120
This is a derived solution

95
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.100
to the photographed exercise, not

96
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.740
a solution visibly present in

97
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.480
the photo. Its proof must

98
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.420
preserve yes and no instances

99
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.580
through both stages. A construction

100
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.040
that balances the sums but

101
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.680
forgets the group counts would

102
00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.680
solve ordinary Partition instead of

103
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.640
the requested target.

104
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.420
Things to take note of.

105
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.200
Handle zero and out-of-range targets

106
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.060
explicitly, remove zeros only for

107
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.440
the existential problem, and preserve

108
00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.480
positivity. Prove both stage equivalences,

109
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.140
count entries as well as

110
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.420
sums, and bound binary arithmetic

111
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.500
and output length before claiming

112
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:34.720
a polynomial reduction.
